Demo 6
Writting a new R data frame to DB
```{r}
#Example- customer_totals
#connecting the customers table to the 'orders' table using matching customer IDs 
#Left join keeps every customer, including customers who have not placed any orders
customer_totals <- dbGetQuery(PSTAT132db, "
                              SELECT c.customer_id, c.name, SUM(o.amount) AS total_spend
                              FROM customer c 
                              LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
                              GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.name
                              ") %>% as_tibble() #take the results from the previous step, then convert it to a tibble 
#as_tible() converts an R data frame into a tibble
customer_totals 

#Disconnect
DBI::dbDisconnect(PSTAT132db)
message("PSTAT 132db Disconnected")
```

Demo 7
Another Example
Creates a temporary (in Memory SQLite Database)
The database exists only while the R session is running 

```{r}
#":memory:" tells SQLite to create a database in memory rather than in a regular database file # a database exists in memory rather than being saved as a normal database file. When the connection closes, its database contents ar elost. 
# The data disappears when the in-memory database is destroyed, typically when its last connection closes 
example7 <- dbConnect(SQLite(), ":memory:")

#Write customer data and pipe as a tibble 
#Creating the data frame with the customers data
customers <- data.frame(
  customer_id = c(1, 2, 3, 4),
  customer_name = c(
    "Alice Johnson",
    "Brian Smith", 
    "Carla Davis",
    "Daniel Lee" 
  ),
  state = c("CA", "NY", "CA", "TX"),
  stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)


dbWriteTable(example7, customers,
             name = "customers", #name of the table to create inside SQLite
             value = customers, #R data frame whose contents should be written into the table 
             overwrite = TRUE #replace existing table named 'customers' if one exists 
             )

california_customers_tibble <- tbl(example7, "customers") %>%
  filter(state == "CA") %>%
  select(customer_id, customer_name, state) %>%
  collect() %>%  #executes this query and brings the resulting data into R's memory 
  #so now, R has the 2 California customers as an actual R data frame 
  as_tibble() #converts the result into a tibble 

california_customers_tibble  
```
